- {"path": "src/sync/local.ts", "issue": "replaceFromRemote lets an equal-ms remote row win and replaces the whole local row. Card.crop is local-only and not in ItemRow, so a pulled row (the device's own pushed row coming back through the 60 s overlap, or any equal-ms row) wipes the local crop. useCardImage then depends on a signed URL and breaks offline. The merge must carry crop over from the local row.", "severity": "high", "line": 0}
- {"path": "src/sync/engine.ts", "issue": "After an upload the local card never gets image_path, and the spec does not say whether that write bumps updated_at. Without a write the engine re-runs shrink, sha256 and the uploaded-list lookup on every push of that card. With a write that bumps updated_at it enqueues a new outbox entry and loops. Specify a local write that stores image_path without bumping updated_at or enqueueing.", "severity": "med", "line": 0}
- {"path": "src/srs/store.ts", "issue": "keep() dedupes locally by (noun_fr, errorType) but the server key is the UUID. Two devices keeping the same noun create different UUIDs, so sync produces duplicate cards. Also, a keep() that returns an existing tombstoned card (all() hides it, but the dedupe lookup may use allWithTombstones) is unspecified: it could resurrect or silently drop the keep. Define the dedupe scope and tombstone behaviour, and document the duplicate limit.", "severity": "med", "line": 50}
- {"path": "src/sync/engine.ts", "issue": "Cursor = max synced_at minus 60 s can move backwards when the new max is below the old cursor plus 60 s, so each run re-pulls the overlap window. Require cursor = max(old, new). Offset pagination (range) over a live table can also skip rows if writes land mid-pull. The 60 s overlap only covers this partly, and it cannot cover a transaction that commits more than 60 s after its now(). Document or use keyset pagination on (synced_at, id).", "severity": "med", "line": 0}
- {"path": "src/sync/local.ts", "issue": "clear() is a hard local reset that enqueues nothing. The Settings 'reset' then leaves server rows in place, and they come back when cursors are cleared (signOut then sign-in, or resetLocal). The user expects deletion. Either state in the spec and the Settings copy that reset is local-only, or tombstone the rows when signed in.", "severity": "med", "line": 49}
- {"path": "src/sync/images.ts", "issue": "shrinkDataUrl returns the input unchanged on a decode or canvas failure. A full-size raw frame is then uploaded silently, which defeats the 1280 px bound the spec is built on and is a silent fallback. Add a size cap, or fail the entry visibly, when the unshrunk data URL is over a limit. The sha256 hash also depends on JPEG re-encode determinism. Dedupe holds only within one browser, which is acceptable but should be documented.", "severity": "low", "line": 0}
- {"path": "supabase/migrations/20261010170000_sync_columns.sql", "issue": "sync_guard returns null on a stale update, so the upsert reports success and the outbox entry is removed. This is fine by design. But there is no server check against an updated_at far in the future, so one device with a bad clock permanently wins every later write. The spec accepts this, but a cheap guard (reject or clamp updated_at > now() + 5 min) would be safer. A real-Postgres test of the trigger is also absent: migrations.test.ts only does text checks, so the stale-skip, the synced_at bump and the on-conflict-updat
- {"path": "supabase/rollback/20261010170000_sync_columns.down.sql", "issue": "The rollback duplicates the set_updated_at body from the init file, and migrations.test.ts only counts occurrences, so drift is not caught. Rollback drops the new columns and their data, and the server then lets clients overwrite newer rows again. A reverted client with leftover synced rows is not addressed. Assert the function body text equals the init body in the test.", "severity": "low", "line": 0}

